  "notes_for_editor": "Thin on wrecks and daily life: no wreck narratives found (corpus Life-Saving hit is a different Sandy Point, Boston). Lighthouse Board reports 1857-1890 and 1883 construction report are not in the local corpus; construction and appropriation history rests on the NRHP nomination (Eshelman 1996) and LighthouseFriends. Disagreements flagged in text: lens (NRHP says original fourth-order Lepaute; LighthouseFriends says fifth-order moved from shore light, fourth-order installed May 1890), red sector (NRHP: by 1890; LF: 15 April 1888), fog signal (NRHP 1938 reed horn; LF says Daboll horn replaced bell 1909, not used in story). 1938 description records one assistant; USLHS roster lists a second assistant (Cherricks) 1926-1944. USLHS lists Wilson D. Rawls as first assistant for 1948-50; LighthouseFriends lists him as head keeper 1948-50. USLHS earliest keeper 1859 vs station established 1858. Maryland DNR page says the tower still houses a 4th-order Fresnel, contradicting the NRHP (lens smashed 1979; replaced by 300 mm acrylic). Reynolds 1895 item is from an Annapolis dispatch to the Baltimore Sun reprinted in the Watertown Republican, cited as that reprint; the Evening Capital line was read on the page. The 1894 report is for fiscal year ended June 1894; printed page not confirmed (scan page cited). Roster 'person' slugs are taken from roster.py for each line. Sandy Point Shoal position in record (39.0159,-76.3846) vs USLHS (39.0095,-76.3842) differ about 0.7 km; OSM position appears right (NRHP 'about 1000 yards east of the beach'). Women: Mary E. Yewell head keeper 1860-61; Alice E. Allen, Mary Jane Whiteford, Mary J. Parkinson first assistants.",
